WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.770
- There are a couple of mistakes
that I see frequently made

00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:06.290
by young graduates entering the workforce.

00:00:06.290 --> 00:00:07.360
Doesn't matter if you work

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:09.280
in consulting, in investment banking

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:11.610
or another high profile corporate job,

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:14.330
these common mistakes of young graduates

00:00:14.330 --> 00:00:16.560
should just be avoided at all costs.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:19.670
From my experience as a
former McKinsey project lead

00:00:19.670 --> 00:00:22.910
today I'm going to talk
about the three top mistakes

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:25.670
that I have seen young professionals make

00:00:25.670 --> 00:00:28.270
when entering the workplace,
when entering consulting.

00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:31.220
I'm going to talk about why
they are so frequently done,

00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:34.530
why they are so bad and what
you should do to avoid this.

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:36.130
Hi guys, my name is Heinrich,

00:00:36.130 --> 00:00:37.760
and welcome to another video

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:40.020
here on my channel, Firm Learning.

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:41.970
It's a bit late right
now, here in Germany,

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:44.030
which is why I'm not
having a coffee break,

00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:45.230
but if you want to enjoy

00:00:45.230 --> 00:00:48.010
a nice coffee watching
this video, then please do.

00:00:48.010 --> 00:00:49.920
As always, if you're taking any value

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:52.380
out of this video at all,
please hit the Like button,

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:53.750
follow the YouTube algorithm

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:55.670
and also subscribe to my channel

00:00:55.670 --> 00:00:57.840
to not miss out on my regular content.

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:01.350
I upload at least one video
every week on every Saturday.

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:02.710
If you want to see even more from me

00:01:02.710 --> 00:01:04.740
you can also follow me on my Instagram,

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:06.360
my handle is @FirmLearning.

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:07.820
And I also have a mailing list

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:10.650
where I share even more
regular insights with you guys,

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:12.120
there's a link to that mailing list

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:13.690
below in the video description.

00:01:13.690 --> 00:01:16.910
But without further ado
let's directly jump into it

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:19.950
and I will talk about the
three mistakes that I see,

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:21.860
and then for each of these mistakes

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:26.060
I'm going to share one true
story that happened to me

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:27.600
while working at McKinsey

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:30.320
which I already showcase as the mistake

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:31.330
that I'm talking about.

00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:34.050
So the first mistake that
I see many people do,

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:36.150
is that young graduates sometimes

00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:39.230
are not really understanding their task.

00:01:39.230 --> 00:01:42.750
If they get their task
they're not really making sure

00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:45.020
that they're a hundred percent aware

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:46.740
of what is expected from them.

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:48.320
And this is of course pretty bad,

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:51.370
because if you do not understand
why you're doing something,

00:01:51.370 --> 00:01:54.250
if you do not fully
understand what you're doing,

00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:58.150
it is very difficult for you
to really get that job down

00:01:58.150 --> 00:01:59.770
in the best way possible.

00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:01.430
And this of course sounds obvious,

00:02:01.430 --> 00:02:03.600
but still, many people do not do it.

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:06.180
And I believe the main
reason why that's the case

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:07.850
is because these young joiners,

00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:09.930
they don't want to seem stupid,

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:12.000
they don't want to be
caught asking something

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:14.680
that they perceive might
be a stupid question.

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:17.730
Which is why they rather not
fully understand the task

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:20.860
than really asking something
two or three times if necessary

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:23.090
to really clarify all the questions

00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:24.420
that they have in their head.

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:26.500
But of course this logic is flawed,

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:28.130
because while for sure it is better

00:02:28.130 --> 00:02:29.860
if you directly understand everything

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:31.660
that you're asked to do the first time,

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:33.150
it is natural that often you won't

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:34.690
and your project lead or your team lead

00:02:34.690 --> 00:02:36.110
for sure will understand.

00:02:36.110 --> 00:02:38.100
And it is definitely much worse

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:39.620
having worked for several hours,

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:41.550
maybe several days on a task,

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:43.290
only then to in the end find out

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:45.920
that you completely
misunderstood the whole thing

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:47.400
instead of just asking a couple

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.280
of extra questions in the beginning

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:50.113
but then in the end

00:02:50.113 --> 00:02:52.450
being able to deliver a great end product.

00:02:52.450 --> 00:02:54.300
So let me share one little story

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:56.760
from my time as a
project lead at McKinsey.

00:02:56.760 --> 00:02:58.500
I had a young associate on my team

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:00.980
who actually was already
an experienced hire

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:02.470
from another consulting firm,

00:03:02.470 --> 00:03:05.520
which is why I kind of expected
that he already kind of knew

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:06.910
what he was doing and had already

00:03:06.910 --> 00:03:08.690
some minimal experience going on.

00:03:08.690 --> 00:03:10.580
So I gave him some excess spreadsheets

00:03:10.580 --> 00:03:12.980
and asked him to do some
very specific analysis

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:14.960
for some specific numbers that we wanted.

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:17.010
And I explained it to
him a couple of times,

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:19.370
and I believed that it was
pretty clear what I wanted,

00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:20.980
and he also gave me the impression

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:23.360
that he clearly understood
what he needed to do.

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:25.410
So then he was working
on that during the day

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:27.930
and indeed it was quite a
complex analysis package

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:29.910
and I checked in with
him a couple of times.

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:31.380
And he always gave me the impression

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:33.730
that yes, everything is
going well and he's on track.

00:03:33.730 --> 00:03:34.950
And then later that evening,

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:37.250
probably around 7 or 8 p.m. on that day,

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:38.640
I wanted to sit together with him

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:40.770
and really review what he has done so far

00:03:40.770 --> 00:03:42.680
just to make sure he
was on the right track,

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:44.700
because I knew that I needed his work

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:46.720
to be sent out later on that day.

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:48.710
So when I really dived into his sheets

00:03:48.710 --> 00:03:50.910
and went into the details
of what he was doing,

00:03:50.910 --> 00:03:54.040
I realized that he just
didn't understand at all

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:55.150
what he was doing,

00:03:55.150 --> 00:03:58.000
and I had the impression
that he also knew himself.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:00.310
Actually he didn't really understand

00:04:00.310 --> 00:04:01.960
what he was supposed to do.

00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:03.550
Because while he did something

00:04:03.550 --> 00:04:06.260
that clearly was not
answering the questions

00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:08.060
that we were asking
ourselves in the beginning,

00:04:08.060 --> 00:04:10.270
and this was far away from anything ready

00:04:10.270 --> 00:04:11.850
to be sent out to a client,

00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:13.784
which is what we wanted
to do that evening,

00:04:13.784 --> 00:04:16.160
and he also knew that this
is what we wanted to do.

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:18.470
So this for me was exactly this example

00:04:18.470 --> 00:04:20.720
of somebody just too afraid, too shy

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:22.650
to ask a clarifying question,

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:25.030
and rather taking the risk
of doing something wrong

00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:27.360
instead of just making sure
that you're really understanding

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:28.193
what you need to do.

00:04:28.193 --> 00:04:30.140
And of course this is
bad, not only for you

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:31.360
but also for your team,

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:33.710
because in that specific case both of us,

00:04:33.710 --> 00:04:35.210
me and my associate,

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:38.220
then ended up working
really late that evening

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:39.700
to get the time to fix it

00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:42.250
and to still be able to send
it out on the same evening.

00:04:42.250 --> 00:04:45.990
The second mistake is to
not think for yourself,

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:48.470
and this is something that I see commonly.

00:04:48.470 --> 00:04:50.260
Not only for young graduates,

00:04:50.260 --> 00:04:53.580
but also for other more
seasoned business professionals

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:56.700
who just do not take
ownership of their work,

00:04:56.700 --> 00:04:59.830
who just refuse to think for themselves

00:04:59.830 --> 00:05:02.360
when they have a certain
task that they need to solve.

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:04.430
Somehow many people seem to believe

00:05:04.430 --> 00:05:08.080
that if they're just exactly
doing what they're told

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:10.380
then they are fine and
then there's no more

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:11.270
that they need to do

00:05:11.270 --> 00:05:13.740
and they're already
doing an excellent job.

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:16.160
This is completely wrong.

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:18.850
While of course it helps
and it is often beneficial

00:05:18.850 --> 00:05:21.000
to do the things that
you are asked to do well,

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:24.250
very frequently, the main value add

00:05:24.250 --> 00:05:26.610
that you can bring to
the table as an employee

00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:29.160
is to think critically about these things.

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:30.940
Is to really ask whether the way

00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:33.720
you have been asked to do
something is the best way

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:36.140
to get to the result that you know

00:05:36.140 --> 00:05:38.970
your team lead, your project
lead, is actually looking for.

00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:42.040
And the truth is that your
supervisor, your team lead,

00:05:42.040 --> 00:05:45.090
he will often be further
away from what is going on

00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:47.970
in the day to day business,
from the nitty gritty details

00:05:47.970 --> 00:05:49.890
of the data that you are working on,

00:05:49.890 --> 00:05:51.200
from all the interviews,

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:53.780
all the conversations that you are having,

00:05:53.780 --> 00:05:57.080
he will be much further away
from that, than yourself.

00:05:57.080 --> 00:05:59.670
Of course he will still try to do as best

00:05:59.670 --> 00:06:02.160
to give you the best guidance that he can,

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:03.970
at least if he is a good supervisor,

00:06:03.970 --> 00:06:06.730
but there is no reason in most cases

00:06:06.730 --> 00:06:09.550
to believe that he can
actually better decide

00:06:09.550 --> 00:06:12.840
what know the right thing
is in a specific instance

00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:15.570
that you have been
working on than yourself.

00:06:15.570 --> 00:06:17.370
So whenever you work on a task,

00:06:17.370 --> 00:06:19.480
make sure that you fully understood

00:06:19.480 --> 00:06:22.020
what it actually is
that you want to achieve

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:24.100
and then if you receive some guidance

00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:26.420
reflect on the guidance and ask yourself,

00:06:26.420 --> 00:06:27.860
whether from the things that you know,

00:06:27.860 --> 00:06:29.680
this really is the best way

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:31.210
to go about certain things.

00:06:31.210 --> 00:06:33.410
And there are lots of
examples of this, right?

00:06:33.410 --> 00:06:34.870
But just something that happens

00:06:34.870 --> 00:06:38.760
pretty much daily in consulting
is creating presentations,

00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:41.200
creating slides and
some of you already know

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:44.440
that I also have a full
course on creating slides,

00:06:44.440 --> 00:06:46.450
like top management consultants.

00:06:46.450 --> 00:06:47.810
If you're interested in that

00:06:47.810 --> 00:06:49.820
check out the link in
the video description

00:06:49.820 --> 00:06:52.780
to my course on creating
slide presentations.

00:06:52.780 --> 00:06:55.510
What frequently happens with presentations

00:06:55.510 --> 00:06:57.700
is that the project lead or the partner

00:06:57.700 --> 00:07:00.350
will somehow sketch out how he believes

00:07:00.350 --> 00:07:02.020
the presentation should look like,

00:07:02.020 --> 00:07:04.640
and then he gives this to
an associate or to a team

00:07:04.640 --> 00:07:06.970
and asks them to implement
this presentation.

00:07:06.970 --> 00:07:08.530
And then what frequently happens,

00:07:08.530 --> 00:07:10.670
especially with less
experienced associates,

00:07:10.670 --> 00:07:14.140
is that they then just exactly
create the presentation

00:07:14.140 --> 00:07:16.090
in the way that they are asked to do,

00:07:16.090 --> 00:07:18.170
exactly in the order of the slides,

00:07:18.170 --> 00:07:20.960
exactly with all the text
as they're asked to do it.

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:23.860
But in most cases you
realize that actually

00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:26.410
it would be better to
arrange it a bit differently,

00:07:26.410 --> 00:07:27.640
that actually it would be better

00:07:27.640 --> 00:07:30.230
to include the information a
bit differently on the pages.

00:07:30.230 --> 00:07:32.440
That maybe showing the things exactly

00:07:32.440 --> 00:07:33.670
that you have been asked to show,

00:07:33.670 --> 00:07:35.310
it just doesn't make sense.

00:07:35.310 --> 00:07:37.650
And, probably, the partner
who asked you to do that

00:07:37.650 --> 00:07:39.440
just wasn't aware of that in the moment

00:07:39.440 --> 00:07:40.490
because as you can imagine

00:07:40.490 --> 00:07:43.160
he has lots of projects
going on in parallel,

00:07:43.160 --> 00:07:46.130
and he's just not that much
into the detail than you are.

00:07:46.130 --> 00:07:49.100
So then these associates
create, still, the presentation

00:07:49.100 --> 00:07:50.680
just the way that they've been asked,

00:07:50.680 --> 00:07:52.140
and then they are so surprised

00:07:52.140 --> 00:07:55.210
that then when they show and
present this to the partner

00:07:55.210 --> 00:07:56.660
that the partner isn't happy.

00:07:56.660 --> 00:07:59.070
Because maybe if he see's
then the final product

00:07:59.070 --> 00:08:01.930
he realizes himself that
it just doesn't make sense,

00:08:01.930 --> 00:08:03.650
and then it's kind of
this weird atmosphere

00:08:03.650 --> 00:08:04.650
where the associate says,

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:07.170
well this is what you
asked me to do, right?

00:08:07.170 --> 00:08:08.120
And then the partner says,

00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:11.270
well yes, but obviously
it just doesn't make sense

00:08:11.270 --> 00:08:14.160
and you recognized for yourself
that it doesn't make sense,

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:15.980
or didn't you see that the whole story

00:08:15.980 --> 00:08:17.350
fits together the way it should?

00:08:17.350 --> 00:08:21.490
So what you should as a good
associate, as a good employee,

00:08:21.490 --> 00:08:23.330
you should ask yourself critically

00:08:23.330 --> 00:08:25.020
how exactly this should be framed.

00:08:25.020 --> 00:08:26.720
And only because you receive something

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:28.100
of the first guidance,

00:08:28.100 --> 00:08:30.900
this, in most cases, doesn't mean

00:08:30.900 --> 00:08:33.200
that you can't make alterations to that

00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:35.240
where you know that it is better

00:08:35.240 --> 00:08:37.030
in the way that you are
proposing it right now.

00:08:37.030 --> 00:08:40.660
So think critically about
the tasks that you receive

00:08:40.660 --> 00:08:42.810
and ask yourself what you can change,

00:08:42.810 --> 00:08:45.020
what you can do better
to really get your team

00:08:45.020 --> 00:08:46.240
the results that they really need.

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:47.790
And of course, if you're doubting

00:08:47.790 --> 00:08:50.117
whether you're idea is better,
then feel free just to ask,

00:08:50.117 --> 00:08:52.690
feel free to just
challenge your supervisor

00:08:52.690 --> 00:08:54.590
just to have a constructive
conversation with him

00:08:54.590 --> 00:08:56.420
and telling him you
know, you sent me this,

00:08:56.420 --> 00:08:59.120
actually I think probably this
other option is a bit better,

00:08:59.120 --> 00:08:59.953
what do you think?

00:08:59.953 --> 00:09:00.786
Do you agree or not?

00:09:00.786 --> 00:09:03.010
Then it shouldn't be a
surprise that in most cases

00:09:03.010 --> 00:09:05.260
if you're having this conversation
in a constructive way,

00:09:05.260 --> 00:09:08.040
the other party will be super
open for your suggestions

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:10.490
and very much appreciate your ownership,

00:09:10.490 --> 00:09:12.530
your input and your perspective in it.

00:09:12.530 --> 00:09:15.700
Now last but not least, let's
get to the third mistake,

00:09:15.700 --> 00:09:19.590
and this is not making
an active effort yourself

00:09:19.590 --> 00:09:23.060
to search out opportunities
for your personal development.

00:09:23.060 --> 00:09:25.760
Somehow many young
professionals seem to believe

00:09:25.760 --> 00:09:28.290
that it is the responsibility of the firm,

00:09:28.290 --> 00:09:30.640
the responsibility of the
company you are working for

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:31.473
to develop yourself.

00:09:31.473 --> 00:09:33.570
And while of course it's
great if your company

00:09:33.570 --> 00:09:35.110
offers these opportunities

00:09:35.110 --> 00:09:38.510
where they really put you on
a development training plan

00:09:38.510 --> 00:09:40.580
to really get the most of you out there

00:09:40.580 --> 00:09:42.030
ready to develop your skills,

00:09:42.030 --> 00:09:44.090
this is not what you should expect.

00:09:44.090 --> 00:09:45.970
And even in companies like McKinsey

00:09:45.970 --> 00:09:49.200
that heavily invest in the
training of their employees,

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:53.080
in the end it is always
the first responsibility

00:09:53.080 --> 00:09:55.920
of the employee himself to search out

00:09:55.920 --> 00:09:57.760
these development opportunities.

00:09:57.760 --> 00:10:00.640
It is up to you to make sure
that you develop yourself,

00:10:00.640 --> 00:10:02.230
it's not up to some manager

00:10:02.230 --> 00:10:03.870
or some company you currently work for.

00:10:03.870 --> 00:10:06.560
And I believe that opportunities
to develop yourself

00:10:06.560 --> 00:10:08.870
really are out there for you just to grab

00:10:08.870 --> 00:10:10.810
pretty much every single day,

00:10:10.810 --> 00:10:14.200
and I just wanna share one
instance when I was an associate.

00:10:14.200 --> 00:10:16.130
So when I was a younger consultant,

00:10:16.130 --> 00:10:17.920
what I did as a young consultant

00:10:17.920 --> 00:10:19.870
to learn about a specific new topic,

00:10:19.870 --> 00:10:21.610
about a new skill that I found

00:10:21.610 --> 00:10:23.420
was somewhat important and relevant to me.

00:10:23.420 --> 00:10:24.900
So I was working on a project

00:10:24.900 --> 00:10:27.690
and trying to identify some
cost saving opportunities

00:10:27.690 --> 00:10:29.720
in a specific area of the company.

00:10:29.720 --> 00:10:33.060
And now we started a new
work stream on the project,

00:10:33.060 --> 00:10:36.600
so a new set of tasks that the
client asked us to look into,

00:10:36.600 --> 00:10:38.610
and this was all about working capital.

00:10:38.610 --> 00:10:41.410
So the company wanted to
improve it's working capital,

00:10:41.410 --> 00:10:43.940
and I always thought that this
was a very interesting topic,

00:10:43.940 --> 00:10:45.350
I wanted to learn more about.

00:10:45.350 --> 00:10:47.860
Because I knew that when
you are in the position

00:10:47.860 --> 00:10:49.843
at some point in the future to
lead a company for yourself,

00:10:49.843 --> 00:10:51.910
it will be just super important

00:10:51.910 --> 00:10:54.300
to understand these
working capital concepts.

00:10:54.300 --> 00:10:57.060
So for those of you who have
no idea what I'm talking about,

00:10:57.060 --> 00:10:58.520
working capital optimization

00:10:58.520 --> 00:11:00.910
pretty much means to
manage your inventory,

00:11:00.910 --> 00:11:03.670
your accounts receivables
and your accounts payables,

00:11:03.670 --> 00:11:05.420
in an optimized and efficient way.

00:11:05.420 --> 00:11:07.560
So from the perspective
of the project lead

00:11:07.560 --> 00:11:10.210
who was responsible for this
whole project at the time,

00:11:10.210 --> 00:11:11.980
he now needed to ask himself,

00:11:11.980 --> 00:11:14.370
okay, to what associate on my team

00:11:14.370 --> 00:11:16.290
do I give this new piece of work?

00:11:16.290 --> 00:11:17.770
And probably from his perspective,

00:11:17.770 --> 00:11:19.890
I wasn't the first person to think about

00:11:19.890 --> 00:11:22.270
because there was another
much more senior associate

00:11:22.270 --> 00:11:25.270
on the team who also had
additional bandwidth at the time.

00:11:25.270 --> 00:11:26.580
So it would have been an easy choice

00:11:26.580 --> 00:11:28.610
for him just to ask this more senior guy

00:11:28.610 --> 00:11:30.900
to take on this additional
piece of work as well.

00:11:30.900 --> 00:11:32.930
But what I did in that instance,

00:11:32.930 --> 00:11:35.300
once I heard that this
new thing was out there

00:11:35.300 --> 00:11:36.690
to do that now we're having

00:11:36.690 --> 00:11:38.690
this additional topic on our project,

00:11:38.690 --> 00:11:41.590
I proactively approached my
project lead and I told him,

00:11:41.590 --> 00:11:43.810
hey this is really a topic
that would interest me,

00:11:43.810 --> 00:11:44.800
and if that's okay for you

00:11:44.800 --> 00:11:46.710
I would really love to take that on,

00:11:46.710 --> 00:11:48.900
on top of the other things
that I'm already working on.

00:11:48.900 --> 00:11:51.250
And of course he appreciated
that and then in the end

00:11:51.250 --> 00:11:53.940
I was the one responsible
for this work stream,

00:11:53.940 --> 00:11:57.280
and I ended up learning
tons of super relevant

00:11:57.280 --> 00:11:58.700
and super interesting things

00:11:58.700 --> 00:12:01.970
that I still benefit from now
today working in corporate.

00:12:01.970 --> 00:12:02.940
So the lesson learned

00:12:02.940 --> 00:12:05.770
is that you need to reach
out to these opportunities.

00:12:05.770 --> 00:12:09.390
The truth of it also is that
pursuing these opportunities

00:12:09.390 --> 00:12:12.840
have some kind of cost,
either monetary or time.

00:12:12.840 --> 00:12:15.040
For me it would have been the easy path

00:12:15.040 --> 00:12:17.883
to not ask for getting this
additional piece of work,

00:12:17.883 --> 00:12:20.700
I had already plenty of other
stuff to do on the project,

00:12:20.700 --> 00:12:22.660
but I wanted to do it so I pursued it.

00:12:22.660 --> 00:12:24.800
Ask yourself as well, what are the things,

00:12:24.800 --> 00:12:27.610
what are the investments
that I can take now

00:12:27.610 --> 00:12:29.910
in this specific instance
to develop myself

00:12:29.910 --> 00:12:32.620
personally and professionally
within the opportunities

00:12:32.620 --> 00:12:33.610
that you have around you?

00:12:33.610 --> 00:12:36.950
So that's it guys, three
common mistakes that I see.

00:12:36.950 --> 00:12:39.200
First, make sure that you fully understand

00:12:39.200 --> 00:12:40.800
the task that you're asked to do,

00:12:40.800 --> 00:12:42.530
second, think for yourself,

00:12:42.530 --> 00:12:45.170
think critically about the
things that you're asked to do,

00:12:45.170 --> 00:12:48.540
and third, actively
pursue the opportunities

00:12:48.540 --> 00:12:49.660
for personal development

00:12:49.660 --> 00:12:51.430
that you find opening up around you.

00:12:51.430 --> 00:12:53.920
